.TH LIBTUIKIT 7 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
libtuikit \- terminal user interface toolkit for full-screen C programs:
architecture
.SH DESCRIPTION
libtuikit is a C11 library for full-screen terminal programs, written so
that the programs of one workspace stop rewriting the same terminal code.
It depends on the C library only \(em no ncurses, no terminfo \(em and
targets xterm-compatible terminals
.RB ( TERM=xterm-256color ).
.PP
It is a toolkit, not a framework: it forces no main loop and no
application object on the program.
The program owns its loop, its model and its view; the library gives it
events, state machines and drawing.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
.nf
  program
    |  events                               drawing
    v                                          |
  tui_term --> tui_decoder --> tui_event     tui_canvas --> tui_screen --> tui_buf --> fd
  (fds, termios,  (bytes -> keys)            (clipped)     (cells, diff)  (bytes)
   signals, size)
          widgets: tui_listview  tui_table  tui_statusline  tui_textview  tui_lineedit  tui_dialog
          helpers: tui_text (UTF-8, width, sanitising)  tui_rect (layout)
          no session: tui_tty (size, raw mode, one key, password), tui_readline
.fi
.PP
.TP
.B tuikit/term.h
the full-screen session; the only module with signal handlers
.TP
.B tuikit/tty.h
descriptor-level helpers that need no session
.TP
.B tuikit/event.h
events and the input decoder (pure)
.TP
.B tuikit/text.h
UTF-8 and display width (pure)
.TP
.B tuikit/buf.h
the output buffer; only tui_buf_write_fd writes
.TP
.B tuikit/screen.h
cells, styles and the renderer (pure)
.TP
.B tuikit/canvas.h
rectangles, layout and clipped drawing (pure)
.TP
.BR tuikit/listview.h ", " tuikit/table.h ", " tuikit/statusline.h ", " tuikit/textview.h ", " tuikit/textedit.h ", " tuikit/dialog.h ", " tuikit/fieldset.h
widgets (pure)
.TP
.B tuikit/lineedit.h
the line editor (pure), tui_lineedit_run and tui_readline (read and write)
.TP
.B tuikit/tuikit.h
all of the above
.PP
Everything that needs no descriptor is a pure function over plain data,
so the interesting behaviour \(em decoding, editing, navigation, layout,
rendering \(em is testable without a terminal.
The renderer writes into a
.BR tui_buf ;
the program writes the buffer to the terminal, and a test feeds the same
bytes to a terminal emulator.
.SH THE LOOP
.nf
tui_term *t = tui_term_open(NULL);          /* NULL, ENOTTY: plain output */
int rows, cols;
tui_term_size(t, &rows, &cols);
tui_screen *scr = tui_screen_new(rows, cols);
tui_buf out;
tui_buf_init(&out);
for (int quit = 0; !quit; ) {
    draw(&model, tui_canvas_of(scr));       /* the program's view */
    tui_screen_render(scr, &out);
    tui_term_flush_buf(t, &out);
    tui_event ev;
    if (tui_term_wait(t, \-1, NULL, 0, &ev) < 0) break;
    switch (ev.type) {
    case TUI_EV_RESIZE: tui_screen_resize(scr, ev.rows, ev.cols); break;
    case TUI_EV_HANGUP: quit = 1; break;
    case TUI_EV_KEY:    quit = handle_key(&model, &ev); break;
    default: break;
    }
}
tui_buf_free(&out);
tui_screen_free(scr);
tui_term_close(t);
.fi
.PP
A program with a network connection or a worker passes those descriptors
to
.BR tui_term_wait (3)
and gets
.B TUI_EV_FD
for them: one loop, no polling interval.
The complete example is
.BR tuikit-demo ,
in the source tree under
.IR examples/ .
.SH THE TERMINAL IS ALWAYS GIVEN BACK
.BR tui_term_open (3)
enters raw mode with every change read back, clears flow control (so
Ctrl-S and Ctrl-Q are keys rather than a frozen screen), and switches to
the alternate screen.
The terminal is restored by
.BR tui_term_close (3),
by
.BR exit (3),
by SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGINT and SIGQUIT (re-raised, so the exit status is
the signal's), and around a stop by SIGTSTP or SIGSTOP and the SIGCONT
that follows (then a resize is reported so the program repaints).
A signal the program catches or ignores itself is left alone.
.PP
A resize is never lost: the SIGWINCH handler writes to a pipe that the
wait polls together with the terminal.
.SH TEXT
Widths come from a table built into the library, generated from glibc's
C.UTF-8 locale, so the program's locale does not matter: in the C locale
glibc's own
.BR wcwidth (3)
reports \-1 for every non-ASCII character, and code relying on it drops
accented and CJK text.
A printable character the table does not know is one column wide.
Every drawing function replaces control characters with U+00B7, so text
from the network cannot carry an escape sequence onto the screen.
.SH EMAIL-CLI EQUIVALENTS
email-cli is the reference implementation the library follows, and the
library may only add to its capabilities.
