.SH TESTING A PROGRAM THAT USES IT
The library's own tests use
.BR libptytest (7):
unit tests render into a buffer and compare the emulated screen with
.BR pty_feed (3)
and
.BR pty_region_compare (3);
the PTY tests run real programs through a real pseudo-terminal.
A program built on libtuikit can be tested the same way.
Every line of the library is run by its tests; failure paths are reached
by link-time fault injection in the test binaries, never by hooks in the
library.
libptytest is a test dependency only; the library shares no code with it.
.SH NAMES
Functions and types are
.BR tui_ module_ verb ;
macros
.BR TUI_ *.
Some programs that will use the library already have symbols with the
.B tui_
prefix of their own; the library never defines
.BR tui_app_* ,
.BR tui_model_* ,
.BR tui_input_* ,
.BR tui_textarea* ,
.BR tui_text_summary ,
.BR tui_form_* ,
.BR tui_api_* ,
.BR tui_run ,
.BR tui_view_t ,
.BR tui_modal_t ,
or the macro families
.BR TUI_EVENT_* ,
.BR TUI_FOCUS_* ,
.BR TUI_VIEW_* ,
.BR TUI_MODAL_* ,
.BR TUI_FORM_* ,
.BR TUI_SUB_* ,
.BR TUI_MIN_* ,
.BR TUI_MAX_* ,
.B TUI_KEY_MAX
and
.BR TUI_INPUT_MAX .
.SH THREADS
One thread drives the terminal.
Other threads wake the loop through a descriptor passed to
.BR tui_term_wait (3).
Signal dispositions are per process, so one session with signal handling
can be open at a time.
